graph_dev internals
===================

This directory is INSIDE the renderer.  Nothing outside graph_dev_opengl.c and
graph_dev_gles.c should include these headers.

The renderer's public interface is ../graph_dev.h, and it is the only part of
this that snis_client, entity.c or a lab has any business calling.  What lives
here is the part the two backends turned out to have in common -- the texture
cache, the mesh cache, the window-level context and 2D drawing, and the shader
setup that is the same on both GL versions.  It is shared so that there is one
of each rather than two, not because it is an interface anybody should build
on.

There is deliberately no mechanism enforcing that.  The directory is the sign.

Why any of it is here at all
----------------------------
graph_dev_opengl.c and graph_dev_gles.c are two implementations of one
interface, and a good deal of what they contained was identical.  Some of that
is genuine: rendering, where two lines that look alike can mean different
things because desktop GL and GLES guarantee different things.  The rest was
not, and two copies of code that must not diverge is a standing invitation for
it to -- one of the differences found while doing this was `if (filled )`
against `if (filled)`.

In every case what actually blocked sharing was state or a macro, never the
code: a context struct that was a file static in each backend, so there were
two of it; shader structs defined twice; a preamble macro that differed by two
string literals.

The seam
--------
Six functions go the other way, supplied by whichever backend is linked:

	graph_dev_cubemap_texture_to_gpu,   GLES spells its sRGB formats
	graph_dev_texture_to_gpu_id         differently and has no
	                                    GL_TEXTURE_WRAP_R

	graph_dev_gen_vao,                  vertex array objects are core on
	graph_dev_bind_vao,                 the desktop and an OES extension
	graph_dev_unbind_vao                on GLES, with their own entry
	                                    points and the possibility of
	                                    being absent

	graph_dev_select_draw_buffer        draw buffers are named explicitly
	                                    on the desktop and cannot be named
	                                    at all on GLES2

Plus one compile-time choice, in context.h: the shader preamble, which is
"#version 150" against "#version 100" and a precision qualifier.  These files
are built once per build with the backend's own flags -- already how they pick
a glad header -- so that stays a compile-time concatenation and no shader
string is assembled any differently than it was.

Keep the seam this narrow.  Anything needing a judgement about which GL is
underneath belongs in a backend, not here.
